<Base title="How the algorithms are made" description="Where the algorithms on this wiki come from and how they are checked.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How the algorithms are made</h1>
    <p class="lede">Most algorithm lists on this wiki were generated by computer search, not copied from other sheets. This page explains how, and how they're checked.</p>

    <h2>Cases</h2>
    <p>Each case list is built by listing every position the step can start from, directly from the pieces, and then grouping positions that are the same case. For example, two positions that only differ by turning the top layer count as one case. That's how you know a list is complete: ZBLL comes out at exactly 493 cases, the standard count.</p>

    <h2>EODFDB and EOStripe</h2>
    <p>These steps only use a few move types (M and U for EODFDB; R, r, M and U for EOStripe), so a computer can search every position completely. Every algorithm listed is the shortest possible in those moves. For LEOR's EOStripe, the move-count table matches the one RedstoneTim published from a different solver, exactly.</p>

    <h2>ZBLL</h2>
    <p>For ZBLL, a solver searched the move sets people actually use for speed: RU, RUD, RUF, RUL and RUr, up to 16 moves. Each case lists up to four algorithms, ordered by a simple rule that favours R and U turns and few half turns. That rule can't feel finger tricks, so the first algorithm isn't always the one most people use. Hand-picked choices from experienced solvers are being added.</p>

    <h2>Checking</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every algorithm is applied to its case on a simulated cube and must solve the step.</li>
      <li>EODFDB and EOStripe lists are checked a second time by a separately written simulator.</li>
      <li>ZBLL is checked a second time with <a href="https://github.com/cubing/cubing.js">cubing.js</a>, the open-source library behind alg.cubing.net.</li>
      <li>The "paste your scramble" lookups are tested against the same data with hundreds of random positions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you find a mistake, or know a better algorithm for a case, that's exactly the kind of feedback that improves the lists.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
